---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Job Fit Agent — Automated Job Posting Triage">
  <article class="project">
    <p class="back-top"><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}projects`}>← All projects</a></p>
    <header class="hero">
      <h1>Job Fit Agent</h1>
      <p class="tagline">An automated triage system for job postings — decides apply, borderline, or skip.</p>
      <p class="meta">Python · FastAPI · OpenAI gpt-4o · 2026</p>

      <div class="actions">
        <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}projects/job-fit-agent/learnings`} class="btn">Learnings</a>
        <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}projects/job-fit-agent/design-decisions`} class="btn">Design decisions</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/alawal1/job-fit-agent" class="btn">⌥ Code</a>
        <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}projects/job-fit-agent/updates`} class="btn">Updates</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <p>Job hunting at scale means reading dozens of postings, most of which are obvious mismatches. The expensive part isn't finding postings — it's the 10 minutes spent reading each one only to realize the seniority is wrong or the tooling is a hard requirement you don't meet.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What it does</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Fetches and extracts</strong> the posting's triage-relevant signals — title, location, languages, seniority, and required vs. preferred skills.</li>
        <li><strong>Applies hard filters</strong> (language, seniority) before spending any judgment on soft fit.</li>
        <li><strong>Assesses fit</strong> against a structured candidate profile, returning a verdict with confidence and reasons.</li>
        <li><strong>Suggests CV improvements</strong> tailored to the posting when the verdict is apply or borderline.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>The agent runs a custom OpenAI tool-calling loop (no LangChain). Each tool — fetch, extract, filter, assess, suggest — is a Python function with a strict JSON schema. The orchestration enforces a required sequence: filters must pass before assessment runs, and assessment must run before CV suggestions. The LLM decides <em>what to do next</em>; deterministic code decides <em>what is allowed</em>.</p>
    </section>

    <p class="back"><a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}projects`}>← Back to all projects</a></p>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .project { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; line-height: 1.65; }
  .hero { text-align: center; margin: 2rem 0 3rem; }
  .hero h1 { font-size: 2.5rem; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
  .tagline { font-size: 1.15rem; opacity: 0.8; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
  .meta { font-size: 0.9rem; opacity: 0.6; margin: 0 0 1.75rem; }
  .actions { display: flex; gap: 0.75rem; justify-content: center; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .btn {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4rem;
    padding: 0.55rem 1.1rem; border-radius: 999px;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.15);
    text-decoration: none; font-size: 0.95rem; font-weight: 500;
    transition: background 0.15s ease;
  }
  .btn:hover { background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18); }
  section { margin: 3rem 0; }
  section h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .back { margin-top: 4rem; opacity: 0.7; }
  .back-top { font-size: 0.9rem; opacity: 0.6; margin: 2rem 0 0; }
  .back-top a { text-decoration: none; }
  .actions { margin-bottom: 1rem; }


</style>